is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router2
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: FRR BGP neighbor configuration on router2. The data plane between routers is healthy (193.10.11.1 <-> 193.10.11.2 ping works), but the eBGP control plane is broken due to an AS mismatch.

Mechanism: When BGP peers exchange OPEN messages, each side validates the remote AS against its configured neighbor remote-as. A mismatch causes the session to never progress beyond Idle/Active, so no routes are exchanged. Without the 195.11.14.0/24 and 200.1.1.0/24 routes learned via BGP, pc1 and pc2 have no inter-AS reachability.

Localization: On router1, BGP runs in AS 1 and expects neighbor 193.10.11.2 to be in AS 2 (show bgp summary: local AS 1; running-config: neighbor 193.10.11.2 remote-as 2). On router2, BGP runs in AS 2 but is configured with neighbor 193.10.11.1 remote-as 65001 (show running-config). BGP summary on router2 shows neighbor 193.10.11.1 stuck in Idle and “never” up.

Root cause: During the router2 change window, the neighbor remote-as was mistakenly set to 65001 instead of 1.

Corrective fix: On router2, correct the neighbor statement to match router1’s AS:
  configure terminal
  router bgp 2
    no neighbor 193.10.11.1 remote-as 65001
    neighbor 193.10.11.1 remote-as 1
  end
Then clear the session (clear ip bgp 193.10.11.1) and verify it reaches Established, with each side receiving one prefix. Afterward, pc1 and pc2 should be able to ping each other. === END ===